  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 318 PM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\n===== This Weekend =====\n\nA large dome of high pressure will reside over the western US the \nbegin the weekend, however that will lead to lingering downstream \ntroughing across the eastern US. This places the Ohio Valley in a \ndeeper NW flow aloft between these two features. Sfc high pressure \nwill have shifted toward the East Coast by this time, which will \nopen the door for precip activity to return, especially for Saturday \nas a stalled sfc boundary across the TN Valley begins to lift back \nto the north. Model soundings do not indicate a lot of CAPE or shear \non Saturday, though PWATs near 2 inches will support some efficient \nrainfall at times. The best flow in the column likely remains at the \nmid-levels or higher, so there likely won't be much low-level \nsteering flow. As such, mainly looking at pulse convection on \nSaturday afternoon, mainly in the I-165 (Natcher Pkwy) area and \nsouthwestward, though still could see some pop up storms across \nsouthern IN and central KY west of I-75. That boundary will continue \nto sit just to our southwest on Sunday, and could be the focus for \nadditional shower and storm activity. Should see more instability on \nSunday, though weak shear is expected to continue. Confidence \nremains lower on Sunday as models continue to vary on convection \ninitiation. \n\nTemps will be heating back up over the weekend, with Sunday being \nthe hotter day. Saturday will feature temps in the low to mid-80s, \nthough Sunday brings a return to the 90s. Dewpoints will be creeping \nback up into the 70s, and which could yield heat indices near 100 by \nSunday. \n\n===== Next Week =====\n\nWe'll remain situated in the NW flow aloft for next week as the \nstrong upper ridging holds over the western US. Temperatures and \nhumidity continue to increase next week, with daily highs in the \n90s, and afternoon heat indices near 100F possible Monday and \nTuesday. That will also support increasing destabilization across \nthe Ohio Valley, which combined with the NW flow, could set up \nadditional severe threats with upstream MCS activity rides down the \nNW flow and/or CAPE gradients and into the Ohio Valley. SPC already \nhas a 15% risk for Monday, which will need to be watched closely. As \nmentioned in the prior discussion, our N/NE portion of the forecast \narea has the best chance for severe activity next week for now, but \nthat obviously could change. \n\nAs mentioned above, heat indices near 100 are expected next week, \nwhich could be borderline heat advisory if we see a few continuous \ndays of heat. This still remains somewhat lower confidence as well \nthough, because any MCS or thunderstorm threat could knock down the \nheat in the afternoon.",
